    },
    {
      "id": "https://www.archdaily.com/1185414/unearthing-the-ground-architecture-and-the-politics-of-waste",
      "url": "https://www.archdaily.com/1185414/unearthing-the-ground-architecture-and-the-politics-of-waste",
      "title": "[ArchDaily] Unearthing the Ground: Architecture and the Politics of Waste",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://images.adsttc.com/media/images/6aaf/fc15/8d91/7901/8920/59e2/large_jpg/unearthing-the-ground-architecture-and-the-politics-of-waste_15.jpg?1789918235\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\"/>\n<p>In 1948, <a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/tag/robert-moses\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Robert Moses</a> established the Fresh Kills Landfill on the western shore of <a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/tag/staten-island\">Staten Island</a>. The site, chosen for its remote marshland and cheap land value, was meant to be temporary, but by 1955 it was the largest landfill in the world, receiving up to 29,000 tons of refuse per day at its 1991 peak. By 2001, after more than fifty years of continuous operation, Fresh Kills covered 2,200 acres, roughly three times the area of <a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/tag/central-park\">Central Park</a>, and was, unspectacularly, visible from space. When it started, Moses didn't expect to design a dump but, without calling it that, a new landform with four artificial hills rising up to sixty meters above sea level built entirely from the accumulated residue of eight million daily lives.</p>",
      "date_published": "2026-10-05T07:30:00+00:00",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "ArchDaily",
          "url": "https://www.archdaily.com"
        }
      ],
      "image": "https://images.adsttc.com/media/images/6aaf/fc15/8d91/7901/8920/59e2/large_jpg/unearthing-the-ground-architecture-and-the-politics-of-waste_15.jpg?1789918235",
      "_score": 45,
      "_quality": 0,
      "_relevance": 0,
      "_local_score": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "https://www.archdaily.com/1185733/the-future-as-propaganda-soviet-architecture-in-kazakhstan-and-the-imagery-of-a-new-era",
      "url": "https://www.archdaily.com/1185733/the-future-as-propaganda-soviet-architecture-in-kazakhstan-and-the-imagery-of-a-new-era",
      "title": "[ArchDaily] The Future as Propaganda: Soviet Architecture in Kazakhstan and the Imagery of a New Era",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://images.adsttc.com/media/images/6ab6/b006/5068/a60f/5eb3/345d/large_jpg/the-future-as-propaganda-soviet-architecture-in-kazakhstan-and-the-imagery-of-a-new-era_5.jpg?1790357521\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\"/>\n<p>Practically every history book records <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/april-1961-first-human-entered-space/?utm_medium=website&amp;utm_source=archdaily.com\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">1961 as the year man first reached space</a>, an achievement that placed the <a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/tag/soviet-union\">Soviet Union</a> at the center of the <a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/928955/the-new-space-race-6-challenges-for-extraterrestrial-architecture\">space race</a>. What not everyone knows is that Yuri Gagarin's journey began in a seemingly unlikely place, more precisely in <a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/search/projects/country/kazakhstan\">Kazakhstan</a>. From the <a href=\"https://www.nti.org/education-center/facilities/baikonur-cosmodrome/?utm_medium=website&amp;utm_source=chatgpt.com\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Baikonur Cosmodrome</a>, Gagarin set off beyond Earth, inaugurating not only a new era of space exploration but also a <a href=\"https://www.archdaily.com/914323/a-soviet-union-of-historic-images\">history of technology and ambition</a> that would transform this landscape.</p>",
      "date_published": "2026-10-05T04:00:00+00:00",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "ArchDaily",
          "url": "https://www.archdaily.com"
        }
      ],
      "image": "https://images.adsttc.com/media/images/6ab6/b006/5068/a60f/5eb3/345d/large_jpg/the-future-as-propaganda-soviet-architecture-in-kazakhstan-and-the-imagery-of-a-new-era_5.jpg?1790357521",
      "_score": 48,
      "_quality": 0,
      "_relevance": 0,
      "_local_score": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "https://www.archdaily.com/1185636/van-peborgh-lodge-claesson-koivisto-rune-architects",
      "url": "https://www.archdaily.com/1185636/van-peborgh-lodge-claesson-koivisto-rune-architects",
      "title": "[ArchDaily] Van Peborgh Lodge / Claesson Koivisto Rune  Architects",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://images.adsttc.com/media/images/6ab3/dad7/7707/e600/01bb/373a/large_jpg/016_Image_Van_Peborgh_Lodge_Photo_A_ke_E-son_Lindman.jpg?1790172207\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\"/>\n<p>The brief said to create a contemporary log cabin in the Swedish mountains. A ski lodge for winter, but as well for all other seasons. To be rooted in (but not a disciple of) the local building traditions of its Dalarna region. And with open views to a landscape that shifts dramatically over the year.</p>",
      "date_published": "2026-10-04T10:00:00+00:00",
